-- s 1054(3)(b), fork F2. The text of the paragraph carries two versions of its last words side by side ("...one-tenth of level 5 on the standard
-- scaleone-tenth of the greater of £5,000 or level 4 on the standard scale."). Taken: the later wording governs, and the two limbs
-- ("a fine exceeding level 5", "a daily default fine not exceeding one-tenth of the greater of £5,000 or level 4") are two separate bars,
-- each worded as a limit the regulations may not go past. The levels are not in the Act: they are inputs, in pounds.
-- src:14842 | (b) for the imposition on summary conviction of a fine exceeding level 5 on the standard scale and, for continued contravention, a daily default fine not exceeding one-tenth of level 5 on the standard scaleone-tenth of the greater of £5,000 or level 4 on the standard scale.
@ref s 1054(3)(b) The regulations must not provide for a fine above the limits (src:14842)
GIVEN `the summary fine the regulations provide for, in pounds` IS A NUMBER
      `the daily default fine the regulations provide for continued contravention, in pounds` IS A NUMBER
      `level 5 on the standard scale, in pounds` IS A NUMBER
      `level 4 on the standard scale, in pounds` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 1054(3)(b) the regulations may provide for those fines` MEANS
    IF       `the summary fine the regulations provide for, in pounds` GREATER THAN `level 5 on the standard scale, in pounds`
        OR   `the daily default fine the regulations provide for continued contravention, in pounds` GREATER THAN ((max 5000 `level 4 on the standard scale, in pounds`) DIVIDED BY 10)
    THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The regulations must not provide ... (b) for the imposition on summary conviction of a fine exceeding level 5 on the standard scale and, for continued contravention, a daily default fine ... one-tenth of the greater of £5,000 or level 4 on the standard scale"
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the bar is a prohibition limited by its own wording, so a fine within both limits is not said to be permitted

